.TH XUR.STD.STUB.HOOKING 7 "2026-10-30" "xur 0.1.2" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.stub.hooking \- low-level inline function hooking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B use std::stub::hooking;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::stub::hooking
provides low-level inline function hooking by address or symbol name.
For Il2Cpp method hooking, prefer
.BR Method.hook() " or " Method.hook_void_block()
from
.BR std::stub::il2cpp ,
which handle ABI marshalling automatically. This module is for hooking
arbitrary native functions where you manage the calling convention
yourself.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B create(target, destination) -> Hook
Create a hook that redirects calls from
.I target
address to
.I destination
address. The hook is not active until
.BR apply() " is called."
.TP
.B by_name(symbol, destination) -> Hook
.TQ
.B by_name(module, symbol, destination) -> Hook
Create a hook by looking up a symbol name, optionally in a specific
shared library. Returns the hook or an error if the symbol is not found.
.SH Hook METHODS
.TP
.B apply() -> ()
Activate the hook. Calls to the target are redirected to the
destination.
.TP
.B remove() -> ()
Remove the hook and restore the original code.
.TP
.B original() -> int
Get the address of the trampoline that calls the original function.
.TP
.B target() -> int
Get the target address being hooked.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::stub::hooking;

// Hook a function by address
let hook = hooking::create(0x7f0001000, my_detour_addr);
hook.apply();
let orig = hook.original();

// Hook by symbol name
let hook = hooking::by_name("libgame.so", "GameUpdate", detour_addr);
hook.apply();

// Remove when done
hook.remove();
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std.stub.il2cpp (7)
